WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:05.114
The cone of uncertainty is a model from Barry Boehm in nineteen eighty-one,

2
00:00:05.276 --> 00:00:07.459
later worked out by Steve McConnell.

3
00:00:08.154 --> 00:00:11.218
The earlier in a project, the wider the range.

4
00:00:12.238 --> 00:00:17.671
At the initial idea it lands at zero point two five to four times the estimate.

5
00:00:18.435 --> 00:00:21.512
Largely expert judgement, not hard measurement.

6
00:00:22.561 --> 00:00:27.495
The cone only narrows if you really remove uncertainty, say with working pieces.

7
00:00:28.192 --> 00:00:29.736
Time alone doesn't help.

8
00:00:31.104 --> 00:00:34.575
A probabilistic forecast gives not a date but a probability,

9
00:00:34.703 --> 00:00:38.952
say an eighty-five percent chance it is done before the first of March.

10
00:00:39.989 --> 00:00:44.250
That's more honest, and the sponsor chooses how much certainty he wants.

11
00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:48.242
The basis is historical data, often the throughput,

12
00:00:48.463 --> 00:00:50.646
the items the team finishes per week.

13
00:00:51.648 --> 00:00:56.431
Four to eight a week with sixty open gives seven and a half to fifteen weeks.

14
00:00:57.754 --> 00:01:00.796
A Monte Carlo simulation draws random weeks

15
00:01:00.843 --> 00:01:03.397
from the real history thousands of times,

16
00:01:03.641 --> 00:01:05.684
until sixty items are done.

17
00:01:06.675 --> 00:01:08.997
That gives thousands of end dates.

18
00:01:09.751 --> 00:01:14.012
You read off when fifty percent of runs are done, and when eighty-five.

19
00:01:14.999 --> 00:01:19.469
Daniel Vacanti and Troy Magennis made this popular in agile teams.

20
00:01:20.188 --> 00:01:23.729
It only works if the way of working stays roughly the same.

21
00:01:25.062 --> 00:01:27.814
NoEstimates took off around twenty twelve.

22
00:01:28.547 --> 00:01:33.575
Stop estimating each item, cut work small and forecast by counting items.

23
00:01:34.605 --> 00:01:37.856
Critics say you need a size for investment decisions.

24
00:01:38.866 --> 00:01:41.084
A recurring problem is Goodhart's law.

25
00:01:41.802 --> 00:01:45.286
When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.

26
00:01:46.369 --> 00:01:51.199
Make velocity or points a target, and people serve the measure instead of the work.

27
00:01:52.266 --> 00:01:57.653
This ties in with estimating in ranges, now from the team's own data, not a feeling.

28
00:01:58.663 --> 00:02:03.632
In the steering committee, ask what data the date rests on and how certain it is.

29
00:02:04.374 --> 00:02:06.882
And what does twenty percent more backlog do?

30
00:02:08.176 --> 00:02:11.903
A team at a packaging-film maker replaces the labelling system.

31
00:02:12.602 --> 00:02:17.420
Forty-eight small items are open, and the steering committee wants a go-live date.

32
00:02:18.485 --> 00:02:22.874
In ten weeks the team finished fifty items, five a week on average.

33
00:02:23.888 --> 00:02:26.791
Ten thousand runs give three in four done within

34
00:02:26.872 --> 00:02:29.566
ten weeks and ninety-eight percent within eleven.

35
00:02:30.287 --> 00:02:32.017
Practically all within twelve.

36
00:02:33.033 --> 00:02:34.461
Which date do you advise?

37
00:02:35.185 --> 00:02:36.323
First a quick sum.

38
00:02:37.055 --> 00:02:41.432
Forty-eight divided by five is nine point six, about ten weeks,

39
00:02:41.548 --> 00:02:42.872
matching the simulation.

40
00:02:43.884 --> 00:02:47.391
The slowest week, three items, gives sixteen weeks,

41
00:02:47.472 --> 00:02:49.678
but every week that slow is unlikely.

42
00:02:50.657 --> 00:02:53.977
Within ten weeks is only about seventy-five percent.

43
00:02:54.706 --> 00:02:59.188
For eighty-five percent certainty, advise eleven weeks as the commitment.

44
00:03:00.234 --> 00:03:04.495
Add that ten weeks is little history, so the spread is probably wider.

45
00:03:05.244 --> 00:03:07.508
If the list grows, the date moves.

46
00:03:08.248 --> 00:03:09.618
We update it weekly.

47
00:03:10.933 --> 00:03:15.496
A service level expectation is, by the Kanban Guide, an expectation.

48
00:03:16.222 --> 00:03:19.334
It says how long an item takes, with a probability,

49
00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:21.969
say eighty-five percent within eight days.

50
00:03:22.984 --> 00:03:25.620
You take it from your own data with a percentile.

51
00:03:26.346 --> 00:03:29.272
The eighty-fifth percentile of cycle times is the

52
00:03:29.353 --> 00:03:32.047
time within which eighty-five percent were done.

53
00:03:33.062 --> 00:03:35.396
That tells a customer more than an average.

54
00:03:36.718 --> 00:03:40.004
With the SLE you steer daily on work item age.

55
00:03:40.718 --> 00:03:45.118
If an item nears the limit, say seven of eight days, discuss it now.

56
00:03:46.139 --> 00:03:47.741
Similar to a control chart,

57
00:03:47.892 --> 00:03:51.770
where you step in before the product falls outside specification.

58
00:03:52.882 --> 00:03:58.037
A cumulative flow diagram shows the items per step per day as stacked bands.

59
00:03:58.788 --> 00:04:01.110
A band getting thicker is your bottleneck.

60
00:04:02.124 --> 00:04:06.141
If arrivals and done drift apart, work in progress is growing.

61
00:04:07.199 --> 00:04:09.625
That touches on conservation of flow.

62
00:04:10.322 --> 00:04:14.571
What starts must on average also finish, a condition for Little's law.

63
00:04:15.881 --> 00:04:19.828
Utilisation is the share of time people or machines are busy.

64
00:04:20.527 --> 00:04:22.141
In the simplest queueing model,

65
00:04:22.233 --> 00:04:26.970
waiting time grows with utilisation divided by one minus utilisation.

66
00:04:27.677 --> 00:04:32.158
Four at eighty percent, nine at ninety and nineteen at ninety-five.

67
00:04:33.181 --> 00:04:35.247
Those numbers hold only for that model,

68
00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:38.104
but Donald Reinertsen showed product development

69
00:04:38.173 --> 00:04:40.879
at high utilisation gets stuck in queues.

70
00:04:41.929 --> 00:04:45.540
A team at a hundred percent has no room for the unexpected.

71
00:04:46.621 --> 00:04:50.731
As a Lean consultant you know this as the reason for buffers and heijunka,

72
00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.634
though knowledge work varies far more than on a line.

73
00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:58.138
Experts argue whether to plan well below a hundred percent,

74
00:04:58.277 --> 00:05:00.796
or reduce variation with smaller work.

75
00:05:02.124 --> 00:05:06.455
A machine-software team at a sorting-machine maker finished twenty items,

76
00:05:06.571 --> 00:05:08.672
on average in eight working days.

77
00:05:09.374 --> 00:05:11.023
But what can the customer count on?

78
00:05:12.039 --> 00:05:14.802
Eighty-five percent of twenty is seventeen.

79
00:05:15.518 --> 00:05:18.316
The seventeenth sorted item took eleven days,

80
00:05:18.467 --> 00:05:22.763
so the SLE becomes eighty-five percent within eleven working days.

81
00:05:23.791 --> 00:05:28.064
One item has been nine days in testing on the machine, near that limit.

82
00:05:28.806 --> 00:05:29.990
Discuss it today.

83
00:05:30.674 --> 00:05:32.694
What holds it up, can someone help?

84
00:05:33.713 --> 00:05:34.967
Look at the tail too.

85
00:05:35.648 --> 00:05:36.681
What did those share?

86
00:05:37.436 --> 00:05:39.491
That is often the real improvement.
